12	// This file guards QueryTxs against a main-chain reorg that makes the chain
13	// shorter while a paginated query is in flight. A first page issued at that
14	// instant must answer from ONE complete main chain — either the old chain or
15	// the new one: the pinned upper height, the whole-range statistics, every
16	// returned record, and the continuation cursor must all describe that same
17	// branch. Once the first page has pinned its range, a continuation either
18	// still sees the complete chain the first page saw (statistics and pinned
19	// height identical) or fails with ErrQueryChanged and a fully zeroed result;
20	// it can never stitch two branches together.
21	//
22	// The scenario (no filter, To left at zero so the first page pins the tip):
23	//
24	//	Retained ancestor (both chains):
25	//	  h1 parent g  txs [x, x] // same id twice in one block: two occurrences
26	//	  h2 parent h1 txs [y]
27	//	Old branch, tip 5:
28	//	  h3 parent h2 txs [a, c]
29	//	  h4 parent h3 txs [d, d]    // duplicate inside one block; block counts once
30	//	  h5 parent h4 txs [e, f, g]
31	//	New shorter branch, tip 4 (replaces h3..h5, rooted at the retained h2):
32	//	  j3 parent h2 txs [p, q]
33	//	  j4 parent j3 txs [r, s, r] // different multiplicity; h5 vanishes
34	//
35	// Old-chain answer (range 1..5): 10 occurrences over 5 blocks, tip 5.
36	// New-chain answer (range 1..4):  8 occurrences over 4 blocks, tip 4.
37	//
38	// The suffix transaction sets are disjoint ({a,c,d,e,f,g} vs {p,q,r,s}) and
39	// every suffix hash differs, so the two complete answers are observably
40	// different not just in their counts but in every record above height 2. A
41	// page that happens to stay ordered (e.g. one new-chain hit followed by one
42	// old-chain hit at consecutive positions) is still a torn answer and must be
43	// rejected by classifyTxPage.
